  "result": "Fixed. `quote a:12.5:2` now prints `27.06`, and all 8 tests pass.\n\n**Cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the fractional part with zeros on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, which turns `\"5\"` into `\"05\"`, so `12.5` was read as 1205 cents instead of 1250. That made the quote (12.05 \u00d7 2) + 8.25% tax = 26.09. Changing it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")` makes `\"5\"` into `\"50\"`, and (12.50 \u00d7 2) + 8.25% tax = 27.06.\n\n**Tests:** None of the existing tests checked `to_cents`, which is how this slipped through. I added `tests/test_money.py`, which covers one, two and zero decimals, a trailing dot, and the \"too many decimals\" error.\n\n**Also found, not fixed:** `to_cents` gets negative amounts wrong. `\"-1.50\"` becomes -100 + 50 = -50 instead of -150. The CLI doesn't take negative prices today, so I left it alone. Tell me if you want that fixed too.",
